    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Company's rule is variously dated from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plassey">Battle of Plassey</a> in 1757, the grant of the diwani of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> in 1765, or the appointment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warren-hastings">Warren Hastings</a> as Governor-General in 1773.</li><li>It expanded by outright annexation and by subsidiary alliances with Indian princes, whose territories made up two-thirds of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in the early 19th century.</li><li>In the Indian rebellion of 1857 almost the entire <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> army revolted, and in 1858 the British government took over the direct administration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> famine of 1769–70, which the Company's uncertain revenue farming may have gravely worsened, between seven and ten million people, a quarter to a third of the presidency's population, may have died.</p></aside>
    </section>

