    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">English East India Company</a> was founded in 1600 as The Company of Merchants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> Trading into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">East Indies</a>. It set up a factory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/machilipatnam">Masulipatnam</a> in 1611, gained rights at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surat">Surat</a> from the Mughal Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahangir">Jahangir</a> in 1612, built a factory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a> in 1640 and leased <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> island in 1668; after the Anglo-Mughal War <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> let it settle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a>. Its beginnings on the coast, among Portuguese, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, French and Danish rivals, gave no sign of what was to come.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-clive">Robert Clive</a>'s victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plassey">Battle of Plassey</a> in 1757 deposed the Nawab of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> Siraj-ud-Daulah in favour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-jafar">Mir Jafar</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-buxar">Battle of Buxar</a> in 1764 forced the emperor Shah Alam II to appoint the Company <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwan">diwan</a>, or revenue collector, of Bengal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Orissa</a> in 1765. By 1773 it was the de facto ruler of much of the Lower Gangetic Plain, and that year it abolished local rule in Bengal and made Calcutta its capital, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warren-hastings">Warren Hastings</a> as the first Governor-General. Historians date the beginning of Company rule to any of these three years.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Land revenue was the government's primary function. The Company inherited a Mughal system in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamindar">zamindars</a> collected rent from cultivators and paid revenue to the state, and its early tax farming may have gravely worsened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> famine of 1769–70. In 1793 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-cornwallis-1st-marquess-cornwallis">Lord Cornwallis</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/permanent-settlement">Permanent Settlement</a> recognised rajas and taluqdars as zamindars and fixed the land tax in perpetuity, at a high level; peasants bore the brunt, forced labour spread, and up to one-third of zamindari lands were auctioned in two decades. In the south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-munro">Thomas Munro</a>'s ryotwari system settled revenue directly with the peasants. In the 1850s Indian revenue agents were found using torture to meet the Company's demands.</p><p>Trade was transformed. Between 1780 and 1860 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> changed from an exporter of fine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> paid for in bullion to an exporter of raw cotton, opium and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">indigo</a> and a buyer of British manufactures. Opium grown under a government monopoly in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges plain</a> paid for Chinese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> and came to make up 40% of India's exports; historian Rolf Bauer says its cultivators were paid less than it cost to grow and the system depended on coercion. Indigo planters coerced peasants as well, until the Indigo rebellion of 1859–60.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>On becoming Governor-General in 1772, Hastings rapidly expanded the Presidency's army, recruiting high-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajputs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a> of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>, the Purbiyas, who came to make up up to eighty per cent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> army. The Company respected their caste rules, with separate dining and no overseas service, and paid them well and reliably out of Bengal's land revenue. Red-coated sepoys under British officers aroused awe in their adversaries, and Indian rulers copied their red jackets.</p><p>The Anglo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> wars (1766–1799) and the Anglo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> wars (1772–1818) left the Company in control of much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutlej-river">Sutlej</a>, and after the Marathas' defeat no native power threatened it. The Bengal army also served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-anglo-mysore-war">Third Anglo-Mysore War</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a>. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vellore">Vellore</a> Mutiny of 1806 the three presidencies' armies numbered 154,500, one of the largest standing armies in the world. Irregular forces were added as territory grew, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> Irregular Force of 1849. The Anglo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> Wars brought the Punjab, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-opium-war">First Opium War</a>, fought to keep the opium trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing China</a>, gained five Chinese ports and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-nanjing">Treaty of Nanjing</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Regulating Act 1773 let the Company act as a sovereign power on behalf of the Crown under parliamentary oversight, gave the Presidency of Fort William supremacy over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a>, and named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warren-hastings">Warren Hastings</a> Governor-General with four councillors. Pitt's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> Act of 1784 created a Board of Control in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, and from then the British government had the final word on senior appointments. The attempted Impeachment of Warren Hastings, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-burke">Edmund Burke</a> from 1788, ended in acquittal in 1795. The Charter Act 1813 ended the Company's monopoly except for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> trade, and the Saint Helena Act 1833 made it an agent of administration and the Governor-General of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> the Governor-General of India.</p><p>The Company ruled annexed lands directly as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> and the rest through subsidiary alliances with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a>, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad-sindh">Hyderabad</a> in 1798 to Bahawalpur in 1833. Hastings set up district civil courts, diwani adalats, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> pandits and Muslim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qazi">qazis</a> to interpret customary law, beside criminal courts and a Supreme Court at Fort William. Persian was abolished as the official language by 1837. A civil service of at most about 600 Britons, many trained at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> College, ran taxes, customs and justice.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Company rule laid the frame of the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-raj">British Raj</a>. Its Supreme Courts and adalats survived until 1861, and its revenue settlements defined land ownership for a century. After 1813 Orientalist policy gave way to Anglicisation: the Asiatick Society of William Jones (1784), the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Benares</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> College (1791) and the College of Fort William (1800), which helped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>, were followed by Thomas Babington Macaulay's Minute on Indian Education of 1835 and the Education Dispatch of 1854. The universities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a> opened in 1857. The Company outlawed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sati-practice">sati</a> and thuggee, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> Widows' Remarriage Act, 1856 gave remarrying widows some legal protection, though very few remarried.</p><p>It also built the first India-wide networks. A public post was set up in 1837 and postage stamps under the Indian Postal Act of 1854; W. B. O'Shaughnessy's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> trial of 1851 grew to 4,555 miles of lines by 1857. The first railway, from Bombay to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thane">Thane</a>, opened in 1853 after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-dalhousie">Lord Dalhousie</a>'s Railway minute planned the trunk lines. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> Canal, built under Sir Proby Cautley between 1842 and 1854, was in Ian Stone's words the largest canal ever attempted in the world.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>British unease over the Company grew from the start. By 1772 it needed government loans to stay afloat while its servants, the "Nabobs", came home rich, and each charter act took away more of its powers. After it lost its trading rights, land revenue became roughly half of government income, yet between 1814 and 1859 the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> ran debts in 33 years, leaving little money for public works.</p><p>Dalhousie's annexations, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berar">Berar</a> in 1854 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Oudh</a> in 1856, unsettled the sepoys of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> army, many of whom lost their perquisites as landed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentry">gentry</a> in the Oudh courts and feared higher land revenue, while service in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> and the loss of "foreign service" pay bred resentment. In the Indian rebellion of 1857 almost the entire Bengal army revolted, and rebels destroyed more than seven hundred miles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> lines; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a> armies and the Hyderabad contingent stayed loyal, and the Punjab Irregular Force helped suppress the rising. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/government-of-india-act-1858">Government of India Act 1858</a> the India Office of the British government took over the direct administration of India in the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-raj">British Raj</a>, and the army was reorganised.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Company rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> began when a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> trading company, after Plassey and Buxar, took the revenues of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> and went on, by war, annexation and subsidiary alliances, to dominate the subcontinent. Regulated ever more closely by Parliament, it reshaped land, trade, law and education until the rebellion of 1857 led the Crown to take India over in 1858.</em></p>

